WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.907
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:10.058
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.358 --> 00:00:17.039
Unit ten: Immigration Policy. Today you will discuss open immigration questions with noun clauses.

4
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:19.841
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.230
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:26.119
Visa system.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:31.218
Visa system means the rules that decide who may enter or stay in a country.

8
00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:35.117
The visa system was changed to attract skilled workers.

9
00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:38.903
Your turn. Say: Visa system.

10
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.116
Visa system.

11
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.025
Border control.

12
00:00:46.017 --> 00:00:49.769
Border control means the checking of people and goods crossing a border.

13
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.975
Border control was tightened after the announcement.

14
00:00:53.583 --> 00:00:56.317
Your turn. Say: Border control.

15
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:00.690
Border control.

16
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:02.974
Pathway to citizenship.

17
00:01:03.983 --> 00:01:08.514
Pathway to citizenship means a legal route by which residents can become citizens.

18
00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:12.370
The bill offered a pathway to citizenship after ten years.

19
00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:16.506
Your turn. Say: Pathway to citizenship.

20
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:21.204
Pathway to citizenship.

21
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.427
Asylum process.

22
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:29.354
Asylum process means the legal steps for people seeking protection from danger.

23
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.507
The asylum process can take several years.

24
00:01:33.117 --> 00:01:36.326
Your turn. Say: Asylum process.

25
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.725
Asylum process.

26
00:01:41.483 --> 00:01:43.111
Immigration reform.

27
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:47.765
Immigration reform means changes made to the laws on migration.

28
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.860
Immigration reform divided the parliament.

29
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.729
Your turn. Say: Immigration reform.

30
00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:59.171
Immigration reform.

31
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:01.301
Labor migration.

32
00:02:02.317 --> 00:02:06.116
Labor migration means the movement of people to another country to work.

33
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:10.260
Labor migration fills shortages in farming and care work.

34
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.199
Your turn. Say: Labor migration.

35
00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:18.568
Labor migration.

36
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:20.968
Integration policy.

37
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:26.456
Integration policy means plans to help newcomers take part fully in society.

38
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:30.269
Integration policy includes language classes.

39
00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:34.360
Your turn. Say: Integration policy.

40
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.898
Integration policy.

41
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.324
Undocumented status.

42
00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:47.590
Undocumented status means the situation of living in a country without legal papers.

43
00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:51.448
Undocumented status makes it hard to find safe work.

44
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.643
Your turn. Say: Undocumented status.

45
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.291
Undocumented status.

46
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.797
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:12.714
Visal, an editorial says 'Whether applicants qualify depends on the visa system.' What is 'whether applicants qualify'?

48
00:03:13.817 --> 00:03:29.182
It is a noun clause acting as the subject. Noun clauses begin with whether, if, what, how, why or that, and they keep statement word order: whether applicants qualify, not whether do applicants qualify.

49
00:03:30.283 --> 00:03:32.469
Can they follow verbs and prepositions?

50
00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:44.310
Yes. 'Nobody knows how many people will arrive.' 'The debate is about what a fair pathway to citizenship should look like.' Notice there is no question inversion.

51
00:03:45.417 --> 00:03:47.788
Why is this useful in policy writing?

52
00:03:48.883 --> 00:03:59.439
It lets us discuss open questions formally: why border control is tightened, whether immigration reform will pass, and who should decide.

53
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.170
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:03.017 --> 00:04:07.768
Grammar focus: Noun clauses: whether and how and what plus clause.

55
00:04:08.068 --> 00:04:09.255
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:13.122
Whether applicants qualify depends on the visa system.

57
00:04:14.017 --> 00:04:17.050
Nobody knows how many people will seek asylum this year.

58
00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.540
The debate is about what a fair pathway to citizenship should look like.

59
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:31.201
A noun clause (whether / how / what / why + subject + verb) keeps statement word order: 'what it costs', not 'what does it cost'. Use 'whether' after prepositions.

60
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.566
Be careful! Don't say: I wonder what does it cost.

61
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.848
Say: I wonder what it costs.

62
00:04:40.148 --> 00:04:42.519
A noun clause keeps statement word order.

63
00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:45.939
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:48.129
The Immigration Debate.

65
00:04:48.429 --> 00:04:51.601
Few issues divide voters as sharply as immigration.

66
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.247
What a country needs from newcomers, and how many it can welcome, remain open questions.

67
00:04:58.233 --> 00:05:06.049
Supporters of immigration reform argue that labor migration fills shortages, while critics worry about how border control can work fairly.

68
00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:13.480
Whether a person with undocumented status deserves a pathway to citizenship is one of the hardest questions of all.

69
00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:19.450
Good integration policy begins by asking what newcomers and communities each need.

70
00:05:20.533 --> 00:05:24.384
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:27.935
Nobody knows blank many people will arrive.

72
00:05:28.235 --> 00:05:31.714
A, how. B, what do. C, that.

73
00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:36.871
The answer is A: how.

74
00:05:38.017 --> 00:05:41.340
A route that lets residents become full citizens is a blank.

75
00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.338
A, border control. B, pathway to citizenship. C, visa system.

76
00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:53.420
The answer is B: pathway to citizenship.

77
00:05:54.583 --> 00:05:56.235
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:56.535 --> 00:06:02.731
A, I wonder what does it cost.. B, I wonder what costs it.. C, I wonder what it costs..

79
00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:09.323
The answer is C: I wonder what it costs..

80
00:06:10.483 --> 00:06:13.121
The movement of workers between countries is blank.

81
00:06:13.421 --> 00:06:18.619
A, asylum process. B, labor migration. C, immigration reform.

82
00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:24.654
The answer is B: labor migration.

83
00:06:25.817 --> 00:06:31.996
According to the reading, good integration begins by asking what newcomers and communities each blank.

84
00:06:32.296 --> 00:06:35.531
A, need. B, owe. C, fear.

85
00:06:39.067 --> 00:06:40.997
The answer is A: need.

86
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.305
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:51.006
What makes immigration policy such a difficult and debated topic in many countries?

88
00:06:59.817 --> 00:07:01.480
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:33.334
Immigration policy is difficult because it touches many values at once: economic needs, security, humanitarian duty and national identity. Businesses may want workers, while some citizens worry about wages, housing or culture. Governments must also respect international law on asylum. Because each group sees different costs and benefits, finding a policy that most people accept is very hard.

90
00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:35.297
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:36.183 --> 00:07:42.435
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun clauses: whether and how and what plus clause.

92
00:07:42.735 --> 00:07:47.860
Today's value is open-mindedness. Listen to new ideas before you judge them.

93
00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:50.557
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:50.857 --> 00:07:54.148
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

